    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "This problem is a total value problem.\nSince at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out, we know that we need to consider cases with 1 book in the library and 1 book checked out, 2 books in the library and 1 or 2 books checked out, ..., up to 6 books checked out.\n\n1. If 1 book is in the library and 1 book is checked out: There are $1 \\times 1 = 1$ way to do this.\n2. If 2 books are in the library and 1 book is checked out: There are $\\binom{6}{2} \\times 1 = 15$ ways to do this. Note that the two books can be arranged in the library, 1 book checked out, or in 1 book checked out, 2 books in the library. We only want to consider the first case, as any other arrangements would double the number of ways.\n3. If 2 books are in the library and 2 books are checked out: There are $\\binom{6}{2} = 15$ ways to choose 2 books out of 6 to be checked out, and there are $\\binom{4}{2} = 6$ ways to choose the remaining 2 books to be checked. So there are $15 \\times 6 = 90$ ways to do this.\n4. If 3 books are in the library and 1 book is checked out: There are $\\binom{6}{3} = 20$ ways to choose 3 books out of 6 to be in the library, and there are $\\binom{3}{3} = 1$ way to choose 3 books to be checked out. So there are $20 \\times 1 = 20$ ways to do this.\n5. If 3 books are in the library and 2 books are checked out: There are $\\binom{6}{3} = 20$ ways to choose 3 books out of 6 to be in the library, and there are $\\binom{3}{2} = 3$ ways to choose the remaining 2 books to be checked out. So there are $20 \\times 3 = 60$ ways to do this.\n6. If 3 books are checked out: There are $\\binom{6}{3} = 20$ ways to choose 3 books out of 6 to be checked out. So there are $20$ ways to do this.\n\nAdding up all the cases, we have $1 + 15 + 90 + 20 + 60 + 20 = \\boxed{196}$ different ways.",
